```apex
/**
 * Pushes newly created Accounts to the ERP, one HTTP POST per Account,
 * so the ERP side can trace every single account individually.
 *
 * Accounts can be inserted in batches of up to 200 (marketplace
 * integration) or one at a time (manual user creation). Since this is
 * triggered from an after-insert context, the callout cannot be made
 * synchronously (uncommitted DML). Using @future(callout=true) would
 * hit the 50-calls-per-transaction limit on larger batches and cannot
 * be chained, so a self-chaining Queueable is used instead: each
 * execution sends exactly one customer and, if more remain, enqueues
 * itself again with the rest of the list.
 */
public class ErpCustomerSync implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

    /**
     * Lightweight DTO carrying only what the ERP needs, decoupled from
     * the SObject so the trigger doesn't have to pass Account records
     * (and so field names can freely match the ERP payload contract).
     */
    public class CustomerRequest {
        public Id salesforceId;
        public String name;
        public String country;

        public CustomerRequest(Id salesforceId, String name, String country) {
            this.salesforceId = salesforceId;
            this.name = name;
            this.country = country;
        }
    }

    /**
     * Shape of a single element in the ERP's JSON array. Kept separate
     * from CustomerRequest to decouple the wire format from the
     * internal DTO, even though today they are identical.
     */
    private class CustomerPayload {
        private String salesforceId;
        private String name;
        private String country;

        private CustomerPayload(CustomerRequest req) {
            this.salesforceId = req.salesforceId;
            this.name = req.name;
            this.country = req.country;
        }
    }

    private static final String ENDPOINT = 'callout:ERP/api/customers';

    private final List<CustomerRequest> pending;

    private ErpCustomerSync(List<CustomerRequest> pending) {
        this.pending = pending;
    }

    /**
     * Entry point used by the trigger. Starts the (self-chaining)
     * Queueable job for the given list of new accounts.
     */
    public static void enqueueCustomers(List<CustomerRequest> requests) {
        if (requests == null || requests.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }
        System.enqueueJob(new ErpCustomerSync(requests));
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (pending == null || pending.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Process exactly one account in this execution so the ERP
        // receives one request per account.
        CustomerRequest current = pending.remove(0);
        sendCustomer(current);

        // Chain the next one, if any remain.
        if (!pending.isEmpty()) {
            System.enqueueJob(new ErpCustomerSync(pending));
        }
    }

    private void sendCustomer(CustomerRequest req) {
        List<CustomerPayload> body = new List<CustomerPayload>{
            new CustomerPayload(req)
        };

        HttpRequest httpReq = new HttpRequest();
        httpReq.setEndpoint(ENDPOINT);
        httpReq.setMethod('POST');
        httpReq.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
        httpReq.setBody(JSON.serialize(body));

        Http http = new Http();
        // Error handling and retries are explicitly out of scope for
        // this ticket; the ERP's 202 Accepted response is not inspected.
        http.send(httpReq);
    }
}
```

I deviated from the "loop + individual insert" approach you described, and here's why:

- **Governor limits**: 150–250 individual `insert` DML statements would consume 150–250 of the 150 DML statements allowed per transaction, so at the upper end of your stated volume (or if this method is ever called alongside other DML in the same transaction, e.g. from a batch or another trigger), it would hit `System.LimitException: Too many DML statements`. That's a production-breaking bug waiting to happen, not just a style preference.
- **Bulk-safe alternative**: Build the full list of `Task` records in memory, then issue a single `Database.insert(tasks, false)` (partial-success mode). This keeps it to one DML statement regardless of list size, and `allOrNone = false` gives us exactly the "one bad record doesn't block the rest" behavior the validation rules require — `Database.SaveResult[]` tells us per-record success/failure so we can map failures back to their opportunity Id.
- I query the opportunities once (`Id, Name, OwnerId`) instead of assuming the caller already has that data, since only `Id`s are passed in — this is also bulk-safe (one SOQL query regardless of list size).
- I skip any opportunity Id that doesn't resolve to an actual record (e.g., bad Id passed in) by adding it straight to the failure set, since no task can be built for it.
- I kept the logic otherwise exactly as specified: `WhatId`, `OwnerId`, `Subject`, `ActivityDate` = today + 7, `Status = 'Not Started'`, `Priority = 'Normal'`.

