# Solution

## Design notes / what I changed and why

The spec says "enqueue one job per case" with `System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(c.Id))`. Taken completely literally, this breaks under the stated real-world conditions: **`System.enqueueJob` is capped at 50 calls per Apex transaction** (`Limits.getLimitQueueableJobs()`), and a single trigger invocation covers the *whole* DML statement — including the nightly 200-record API close. Calling `enqueueJob` once per case in that scenario throws `System.LimitException: Too many queueable jobs added to the queue`.

To honour the intent (one case, one job, isolated failures) while staying bulk-safe for both a 5-case list-view close and a 200-case nightly API close, I did the following:

1. **Trigger (`CaseTrigger`)** still filters for the not-closed → closed transition and, whenever the batch fits inside the remaining Queueable allowance for the current transaction (checked dynamically with `Limits.getQueueableJobs()` / `Limits.getLimitQueueableJobs()`, so it adapts automatically if the trigger is ever fired from inside another async context with a tighter limit), it enqueues one `CaseSurveyJob` per case exactly as requested.

2. When there isn't enough room (e.g. 200 cases, limit 50), the trigger uses all-but-one of the remaining slots for individual per-case jobs, and packs the *rest* of the case Ids into a single "overflow" job. That job processes one case at a time and, after finishing, **re-enqueues itself with the remaining Ids** (`System.enqueueJob` called once from inside `execute()` is allowed as a chain, and chaining doesn't count against the 50-per-transaction cap). The net effect: every case still gets its own isolated execution/Task creation and a failure on one case can't affect any other case — we just spread the enqueues over a few extra async hops instead of blowing the limit in one shot.

3. **`CaseSurveyJob`** re-queries the case (`CaseNumber`, `ContactId`) instead of trusting data passed in from the trigger, so each execution is self-contained and correct even after the chain hands it off. If there's no contact, it simply creates no `Task` (per spec) but the job still "handles" the case (so the per-case isolation guarantee holds uniformly). Task creation is wrapped so that a DML failure on one case doesn't stop the chain from continuing to the rest.

4. Both classes are `without sharing`: this is a system-level background process (sending a survey after close shouldn't depend on which agent happened to close the case), and triggers already run in system context, so the async job should behave consistently regardless of the running user's record access.

File: force-app/main/default/triggers/CaseTrigger.trigger
```apex
/**
 * After-update trigger on Case.
 *
 * For every case that transitions from not-closed to closed, we kick off a
 * CaseSurveyJob (Queueable) that creates a CSAT survey Task. Each case gets
 * its own job execution so that a failure on one case never affects another.
 *
 * System.enqueueJob is capped at 50 calls per Apex transaction
 * (Limits.getLimitQueueableJobs()). Agents can bulk-close from list views and
 * a nightly API job can close up to 200 cases in a single DML statement /
 * single trigger invocation, so we can't always call enqueueJob once per
 * case directly without breaching that limit. Instead:
 *   - If the whole batch fits in the jobs still available this transaction,
 *     we enqueue one CaseSurveyJob per case directly (the simple, requested
 *     behaviour).
 *   - Otherwise we use up the remaining slots for individual cases and hand
 *     the rest to a single "overflow" CaseSurveyJob that processes one case
 *     at a time and re-enqueues itself with what's left (chaining). Chaining
 *     from inside an executing Queueable job only ever adds a single job and
 *     does not count against the 50-per-transaction limit, so this safely
 *     handles the 200-case nightly case regardless of how large it gets,
 *     while still giving every case its own isolated execution.
 */
trigger CaseTrigger on Case (after update) {

    List<Id> closedCaseIds = new List<Id>();

    for (Case newCase : Trigger.new) {
        Case oldCase = Trigger.oldMap.get(newCase.Id);
        if (newCase.IsClosed && !oldCase.IsClosed) {
            closedCaseIds.add(newCase.Id);
        }
    }

    if (closedCaseIds.isEmpty()) {
        return;
    }

    Integer jobLimit = Limits.getLimitQueueableJobs();
    Integer jobsUsed = Limits.getQueueableJobs();
    Integer availableSlots = jobLimit - jobsUsed;

    if (availableSlots <= 0) {
        // No room left in this transaction to enqueue anything further
        // (e.g. other automation already used up the allowance). There is
        // nothing safe we can do here without risking a LimitException.
        return;
    }

    if (closedCaseIds.size() <= availableSlots) {
        // Plenty of room: one job per case, exactly as specified.
        for (Id caseId : closedCaseIds) {
            System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(caseId));
        }
        return;
    }

    // Not enough room for one job per case in this single transaction.
    // Use all but one of the remaining slots directly...
    Integer directSlots = availableSlots - 1;
    for (Integer i = 0; i < directSlots; i++) {
        System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(closedCaseIds[i]));
    }

    // ...and hand the remainder to a single chaining job that works through
    // them one case at a time without ever breaching the queueable limit.
    List<Id> remaining = new List<Id>();
    for (Integer i = directSlots; i < closedCaseIds.size(); i++) {
        remaining.add(closedCaseIds[i]);
    }
    System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(remaining));
}
